+# Deterministic invariant regressions
+
+The corrected profile is maintained in `../profiles/task-definition.yaml`.
+This change starts from consumer commit `040fb2b740f52459a8e55cbb0fe2e08ee691109b`.
+That profile is byte-identical to the supplied handoff baseline, SHA256
+`57d8498e8fc14641ffef404bd48d4f5604d49ac45a07419b08dbd2c8010f6cdc`.
+The written skill and authoring/review guidance retain their existing authority.
+
+Use Markdown Engine implementation commit
+`fb9aa69bac5d12bdb860b985a3dc6bc48027e267` (development build reporting `3.5.0`),
+based on released source `ec9c5bfe5ecb9c5c8cc4a075f5d4e46e599fcd30`.
+The release version alone does not identify these new capabilities. Unmodified
+3.5.0 rejects the new profile at configuration validation; it must not be used
+as the fixed runtime. No wrapper installation, release, or fleet activation is
+part of this delivery.
+
+From the consumer repository, run:
+
+```sh
+MARKDOWN_ENGINE_BIN=/absolute/engine/dist/cli/index.js \
+  bash skills/task-definition/tests/run-profile-validation.sh
+```
+
+The existing lifecycle, metadata, criterion/evidence and checkpoint regressions
+run first; the invariant matrix then runs through the same ordinary `validate`
+CLI. Set `TASK_DEFINITION_INVARIANT_REPORT` to retain the matrix JSON.
+Set `TASK_DEFINITION_PROFILE_REPORT_DIR` to retain existing-suite CLI JSON and
+actual exit-status files. These reporting options do not validate documents or
+supply rejections. Python 3.9+ and Node are required by the test harness.
+
+For an isolated matrix run:
+
+```sh
+python3 skills/task-definition/tests/run-invariants.py \
+  --engine /absolute/engine/dist/cli/index.js --output /tmp/invariants.json
+```
+
+The six files in `fixtures/invariants` are the unchanged supplied reproduction.
+`invariant_cases.py` constructs isolated structural mutations and additional
+positive controls; `run-invariants.py` records file SHA256 hashes, engine version,
+exit status, validity, and diagnostics, and checks the intended rule for each
+negative. The proof text and commit names inside fixtures are fictional test data.
+
+| Invariant | Capability ownership |
+| --- | --- |
+| One control authority table and row, allowing a separate BLOCKED detail table | Existing `selectionCount` and table-header selectors; consumer rules |
+| Optional checkpoint with one correctly placed heading/table/row | Existing scoped selectors, `when`, `selectionCount`, `tableColumnsExact`; global uniqueness makes the table selected in both Notes and Checkpoint the same table |
+| Allowed checkpoint statuses and a reference or the legal no-evidence sentinel | Existing `anyOf`, literal text/length, row predicates and ID count assertions; consumer vocabulary |
+| Visible non-whitespace checkpoint cells | New opt-in v2 `text.nonBlank`; generic node-text predicate, excludes raw HTML and preserves literal code/Markdown text |
+| Declared checkpoint evidence IDs | Existing `tableColumnCoverage` direction reversed, with new explicit `allowEmptySource` policy for the sentinel |
+| Required criterion coverage only when review-ready | Existing conditional row predicate and coverage target scoping, with new `source.rowWhere` sibling filter and empty-source policy for all-optional criteria |
+
+`Yes` identifies required criteria; `No` identifies optional criteria, matching
+the controlling examples. Filtering uses normalized cell text, so emphasis and
+inline-code formatting are preserved. No evidence serialization format is added.
+The rule checks only references in `Evidence admitted`; IDs in Next action or
+Validation / Evidence cannot supply missing checkpoint coverage.
+
+Structural reference coverage does not establish evidence truth, freshness,
+per-claim sufficiency, implementation completion, or acceptance. Those judgments
+remain semantic review responsibilities. `text.nonBlank` checks normalized text
+outside raw HTML; it does not evaluate CSS or external rendering.
